<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The Reformation and Its Wars</h1><p class="path-tagline">How Luther's protest of 1517 split Western Christianity and opened a century of religious wars.</p><p class="path-intro">In 1517 Martin Luther wrote his Ninety-five Theses against the sale of indulgences, and Western Christianity split into Lutheran, Reformed, Anglican and Anabaptist churches. Taken up or resisted by princes and cities, the new faiths became bound up with politics and war. This path follows the reformers, the Catholic answer of the Council of Trent, the settlements of Augsburg and Nantes and the wars around them, from the German Peasants' War to the Thirty Years' War, which the Peace of Westphalia ended in 1648.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Thinker</a> · <a href="/start">the Strategist</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/movement/protestant-reformation"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-luther.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · 1517 CE – 1648 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Protestant Reformation</span><span class="path-step-why">Begun by Luther's protest against indulgences in 1517, it taught salvation by grace through faith alone and broke the unity of Western Christianity.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Protestant Reformation was the religious movement of the sixteenth century, begun by Martin Luther in 1517, that broke with the Roman Catholic Church and created Protestant churches.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-luther.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1483–1546</span><span class="path-step-name">Martin Luther</span><span class="path-step-why">The German monk whose Ninety-five Theses began the Reformation, who refused to recant before Charles V at Worms in 1521 and translated the Bible into German.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Martin Luther was a German theologian and monk whose Ninety-five Theses of 1517 began the Protestant Reformation.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1524–1525</span><span class="path-step-name">German Peasants' War</span><span class="path-step-why">Partly inspired by the Reformation, peasants and townspeople rose in 1524–1525 demanding an end to serfdom; the princes' armies killed perhaps 100,000 people.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The German Peasants' War was a widespread rising of peasants and townspeople in southern and central Germany, Austria and Switzerland in 1524–1525.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-calvin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1509–1564</span><span class="path-step-name">John Calvin</span><span class="path-step-why">The French reformer whose Institutes became a founding text of Reformed Protestantism, and whose Geneva became a model and training centre for Reformed churches.</span><span class="path-step-lede">John Calvin was a French theologian and reformer whose Institutes of the Christian Religion became a founding text of Reformed Protestantism.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/movement/counter-reformation"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/counter-reformation.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · 1545 CE – 1648 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Counter-Reformation</span><span class="path-step-why">The Catholic answer: the Council of Trent (1545–1563) restated doctrine and reformed the clergy, while the Jesuits carried missions to the Americas and Asia.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Counter-Reformation was the period of Catholic renewal and response to the Protestant Reformation, from the mid-16th to the mid-17th century.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · 800 CE – 1806 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Holy Roman Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Its fragmentation let the Reformation survive, and the Peace of Augsburg of 1555 let each ruler choose Catholicism or Lutheranism for his lands.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Holy Roman Empire was a complex political union of territories in central Europe that existed from 962 to 1806.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/st-bartholomews-day-massacre"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-bartholomews-day-massacre.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1572</span><span class="path-step-name">St. Bartholomew's Day Massacre</span><span class="path-step-why">The killing of Huguenots in Paris and other French cities in 1572, during the French Wars of Religion, a year before Poland-Lithuania made religious tolerance law.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The St. Bartholomew's Day Massacre was the killing of Huguenots in Paris and other French cities in 1572, during the French Wars of Religion.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-nantes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edict-of-nantes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1598</span><span class="path-step-name">Edict of Nantes</span><span class="path-step-why">Henry IV ended the French Wars of Religion in 1598 by granting the Huguenots freedom of conscience and fortified towns; Louis XIV revoked it in 1685.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Edict of Nantes was issued by Henry IV of France in April 1598 to end the French Wars of Religion.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1618–1648</span><span class="path-step-name">Thirty Years' War</span><span class="path-step-why">Beginning in 1618 as a religious struggle within the Holy Roman Empire, it widened into a contest among the major European powers and devastated much of Germany.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Thirty Years' War was a series of conflicts fought mainly in central Europe from 1618 to 1648, beginning as a religious struggle within the Holy Roman Empire and widening into a contest among the major European powers.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1648</span><span class="path-step-name">Peace of Westphalia</span><span class="path-step-why">The treaties of 1648 that ended the war, confirmed the princes' power over religion in their lands, recognised Dutch independence and shaped the idea of territorial sovereignty.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Peace of Westphalia was the series of treaties signed in 1648 that ended the Thirty Years' War.</span></span></a></li></ol>
